  "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x & =-2 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}\u2061x & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we have to find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}\u2061(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/assets/2e1f1ef6c82e30b5f9a591fca432914562f949a7ed5cabc3c4eb92bfecf899ee.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ is not in the required domain $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi .$ To get a solution that is inside the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}-2\\pi =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3},-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot (-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$",
